WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.730
Hiyoshi Liquor Store

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:03.650
Hiyoshi Liquor Store

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.200
Agricultural 
University First-year

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.200
Sawaki Souemon Tadayasu

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.950
Agricultural 
University Second-years

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.950
Misato Kaoru

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.950
Kawahama Takuma

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.870
Agricultural University First-year On Temporary Leave  Yuuki Kei (♂)

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:26.820
Sawaki?

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.380
Kei.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:29.040
That's Kei.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.280
For real?!

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.960
So he took a leave of absence 
from school to dress as a girl?

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:34.510
Uh...

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.810
Hiyoshi Liquor Store Owner's Eldest Son  Hiyoshi Tomoharu

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.800
What are you doing?! Are you stupid?!

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.660
Well, you said you're going to 
turn this into your ideal store,

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.310
but you never do anything about it.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.900
Still, what you're doing 
will ruin this store!

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.370
Wow. You've really done 
a number on this place.

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.370
Agricultural 
University 
Professor

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:56.370
Itsuki 

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.460
This means you'll be 
closed for some time.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.840
Well, even if the store takes a break,

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.870
we'll be fine since we can still 
make money from the bar in back.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.750
Hiyoshi 
Liquor 
Store 
Owner

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.750
Hiyoshi 

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.750
But you understand, don't you?

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:08.000
A liquor store is not merely 
a place to sell liquor.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.500
<i>He's still a microbe monster.</i>

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.000
Tadayasu's Eye

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.000
This is How He Looks to Tadayasu.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.090
Long time, no see! Moyashimon here!

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.140
This story is about a boy

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.820
at an agricultural university 
who can see microbes,

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:23.020
Sawaki Souemon Tadayasu, and his friends.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.340
We microbes will also pop in

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.540
with our own minimalistic 
theater presentations!

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.020
We're gonna brew up some fun again!

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.020
Let's brew!

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:38.490
Moyashimon

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.490
Returns

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.540
A. oryzae

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.540
A. sojae

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.510
It's autumn, a very busy season 
for both farms and microbes.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.540
Livestock coming through!

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:10.750
Priority passage requested!

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:12.750
Agricultural Department's Fermenting Cellar

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.790
It's just about time for Itsuki's seminar 
to start up its fermenting cellar.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:18.280
They make all kinds of 
fermented products here.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:19.970
We're gonna brew!

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.720
Fermenting Cellar Back Door

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:25.560
Agricultural University First-year  Oikawa Hazuki

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:25.550
Professor Itsuki?

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.760
My yogurt is delicious.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.180
L. bulgaricus

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.230
Hey, Professor?

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:33.910
Weird...

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:35.980
I know I caught a glimpse of him.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.990
Has there always been an opening here?

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.230
I don't want to go down there alone.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.250
You idiot! You idiot!

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.750
You're not supposed to really 
swing a whip at people like that!

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:56.220
Shut up!

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.920
Agricultural University Graduate Student  Hasegawa Haruka

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.500
He deserves it for spreading stupid rumors,

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:58.770
even after all this time!

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.150
I still have my doubts 
about what you two did

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:03.510
that night during the Spring Festival!

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:04.970
Are you that stupid?!

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.410
Besides, you told everyone

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.100
what happens when I get 
drunk, didn't you?!

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.630
Do you like raw meat?

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:18.760
Th-That's ridiculous!

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:19.730
Don't lie to me!

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.130
Damn.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.450
Uh, according to my investigations,

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:32.680
scattered rumors of 
that Spring Festival night

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:33.370
Investigation 
Notes

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.810
point to the conclusion 
that a drunken Hasegawa-san

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:37.630
forced herself upon Misato-kun 
in the heat of a moment.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.630
Agricultural University Third-year

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.630
Mutou 

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.020
And did you deny them?

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.750
Well, no... I don't know the truth, so...

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.260
Aya-chan!

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.600
Agricultural University Third-year  Hirooka Aya

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.140
What do you think of those rumors

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:48.730
about Hasegawa-san and Misato-kun?

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:51.640
Oh, I really can't say.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.230
I'll tell her! I'll go tell her!

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.650
Oh, Professor! 
I was looking for you earlier!

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.060
You were? Sorry about that.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:05.030
Hey, guys!

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:07.560
You're Yuuki-kun, right?

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:08.330
Yeah.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:09.030
Yuuki?!

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:11.370
I had heard rumors, but...

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.420
So, this is what his 
transformation looks like...

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:15.650
Isn't he cute? Isn't he cute?

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:17.610
He's Sawaki-kun's girlfriend now!

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:18.540
No, no, no...

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:21.340
Anyway, is the liquor store okay?

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.520
Yeah, they're remodeling now.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.420
I came here to help out 
until they're done.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.550
Sorry for all that ruckus the other day.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.530
Actually, I was more 
surprised by your outfit.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:32.500
Yeah.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:34.960
Well, everyone, 
if we can get right to it,

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.060
let's split into the rice 
team and the soybean team.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.370
We'll use the soybeans 
for soy sauce and miso,

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:42.700
and I was thinking of polishing 
the rice to make sake.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:44.760
Now, which team would everyone 
like to work on?

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:45.790
Beans!

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:45.790
Beans!

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:46.590
Support for the bean team!

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:47.600
Directing the bean team!

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:50.650
Why does everyone want to 
work with the soybeans?

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.780
Wait, doesn't polishing 
rice usually involve

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.030
using a water wheel to grind 
the rice for several days

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:56.120
with a millstone?

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:56.960
What?!

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:59.140
Sorry, Oikawa. 
We'll leave the rice to you.

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:01.440
No! You're a man, 
aren't you?! Trade me!

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:02.950
Men don't want to do it either!

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:05.090
How long do you think it 
takes to polish rice by hand?!

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:07.810
That mallet in the professor's hand means

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:10.660
the uneducated first-years 
belong on the rice team!

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.350
Pound the living hell out of that rice!

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:15.800
We won't be using the mallet.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.600
This is for making New Year's mochi.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:20.500
All right, we'll head 
to the rice mill now.

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:21.890
Take care of the rest.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.530
Thank goodness we don't have 
to polish that rice by hand!

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:30.140
That would definitely be exhausting.

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:31.030
Hey...

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:32.500
Did you take a leave of absence

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:35.790
so you could work at 
Hiyoshi's liquor store?

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:37.980
No, that's not why.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.160
Then, so you could dress like a girl?

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:42.540
That might be closer to the truth.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.170
Hey, you're going to give 
people the wrong idea.

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:47.050
Shouldn't you give a clearer explanation?

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:51.300
Well, I guess you could say I wanted 
to try all kinds of new things.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:54.300
So you had a lot of things 
on your mind, huh?

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:55.810
Yeah.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.090
By the way, this year

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:01.470
we harvested six bales, 
or 360 kilograms of brewing rice,

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:04.060
which is why I thought 
we'd use it all to make sake.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:06.730
What polishing ratio should we use?

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:09.820
Well, we can't expect 40%, 
with the amount we have.

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.450
Let's try for 50%.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:14.090
That's more than good enough!

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:17.390
A 50% polishing ratio would 
give us daiginjo sake!

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:17.390
Daiginjo is a premium sake, made by 
polishing away at least 50% of the rice.

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:18.870
Polishing ratio?

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:29.260
Example: When rice is 60% polished

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:29.260
Polished Away

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:29.260
Rice

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:29.260
60% becomes "rice bran"                                 Remaining 40% is "rice"

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.230
The "polishing ratio" in 
terms of making Japanese sake

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:26.130
is the percentage of rice 
that is polished away

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.290
to produce rice suitable for making sake.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:30.410
There is also a percentage to 
express the amount polished away,

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:35.680
Example: When rice is 60% polished

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:35.680
Polished Away

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:35.680
Rice

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:35.680
60% becomes "rice bran"                            Remaining 40% is "rice"

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:35.680
Polishing ratio:           40%  

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:31.750
but that means the opposite.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:33.150
That's what we call the percentage

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.610
of rice that gets polished away.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:35.680
That is the opposite.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:37.920
So, why do we polish the rice down?

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:41.190
In short, polishing the rice 
leads to better tasting sake.

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:51.950
White 
Core

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:45.060
The white core of a rice 
grain is high in starch,

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:48.210
and that is what we 
value when making sake.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:49.970
The white core is larger in brewing rice

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:52.110
than in rice used for eating.

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:58.700
Protein
 Fat 
Vitamins etc. 
... 
Known as 
"savory" 
in foods. 
Known as 
"bitterness" 
in making 
sake.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:53.860
The other nutrients 
surrounding the white core

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:56.890
produce several different 
flavors when made into sake,

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:58.700
so it's sometimes called 
the sake's "bitterness."

00:07:58.700 --> 00:07:59.580
Wow...

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:03.460
We don't have our own rice 
paddies at our brewery,

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:05.880
so we purchase rice that's 
already been polished

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:07.840
and use it to make our sake.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.650
So this will be my first 
time seeing a rice mill.

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:11.830
I'm so excited!

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:22.390
What do you think, Yuuki-kun?

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:25.100
Thanks to the creation of 
these upright rice mills,

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:28.110
we can polish away as much 
of the rice as we want.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:31.630
Yes. In a way, if these 
hadn't been invented,

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:33.610
Ginjo is another premium type of sake, 
made from rice with over 60% polished away.

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:33.610
we wouldn't have ginjo sake.

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:37.250
But polishing away half of the rice?

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:39.370
Sake sure is a luxury item.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:41.740
Indeed it is, in fact,

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:43.060
I have met many people who

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:45.400
said they don't want 
to drink daiginjo sake.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:53.880
Bought with my 
first paycheck. 
-Your daughter

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:53.880
It's too 
luxurious, 
so I won't 
drink it. 
-Dad

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:53.880
Daiginjo

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:47.690
They believe that polishing 
away and discarding

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:49.760
more than half of that 
hard-earned rice harvest

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:53.880
and valuing the daiginjo sake that 
gets made with it is overindulgent.

00:08:54.390 --> 00:08:56.190
But you know what I think?

00:08:56.190 --> 00:09:00.890
Nobody would wear a diamond ring 
with the pure, uncut diamond.

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.130
You may think, "So you're 
saying both diamonds and

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:06.670
daiginjo sake are overindulgent 
luxury items,"

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:08.900
but anyone can reword another's opinion.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:14.300
Is it wrong to admire something 
that gains its shine by being polished?

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:18.460
Still, common sake can 
be delicious, as well,

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:21.780
and people who only drink daiginjo 
can be somewhat disagreeable.

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:24.870
I think people should just enjoy 
drinking all types of sake.

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:31.690
However, if I may offer a rebuttal 
as a man of agriculture...

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:35.920
We do not simply throw anything away.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:38.600
We give thanks for the 
bounties we harvest,

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:40.660
and respond through our deeds.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:44.430
I believe that both brewers 
and farmers should do this.

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:49.440
We're back!

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:51.780
Oh... Welcome back.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:52.570
Whoa...

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:53.770
What happened to you?

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:56.490
We've been in this cellar steaming 
soybeans the whole time.

00:09:57.610 --> 00:10:00.230
You have to soak the beans 
in water before steaming them,

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:02.030
so they're really heavy.

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:04.200
I should've gone with the rice team.

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:07.070
Are Misato-san and 
Kawahama-san in the cellar?

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:07.740
Huh?

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:11.710
Yep. They're by the steamer, 
steaming along with the beans.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:15.250
<i>That opening is blocked by stuff.</i>

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:16.810
Oh, hey, Oikawa.

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:18.040
Yes?

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:21.130
There's some yeast on my 
desk on the second floor.

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:22.320
Could you go get it?

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.190
It's faster to go down the hall 
rather than the front entrance.

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:27.600
Okay.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:29.140
Fermenting Cellar

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:32.090
I wonder if Hasegawa-san doesn't 
know about that opening?

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:32.480
Fermenting 
in Progress 
-Mutou

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:32.480
Making 
Natto 
-Mutou

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.580
It couldn't have been 
my imagination, right?

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:37.570
All Doors Must 
Remain Closed 
-Mutou

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:37.210
I know I saw it.

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:38.110
Oh, well.

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:39.990
I'll find out eventually.

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:42.910
Brewing, huh? How exciting!

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:47.570
The microbes have now 
divided into three nations,

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:49.480
and those within the 
intestines are exhausted.

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:53.880
But we continue to fight 
because we are proud microbes!

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:57.920
Malt

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:58.040
Now is our time to proceed toward 
battle as soy sauce and miso,

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:00.590
suppress those within the intestines,

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:02.390
and make the intestinal flora our own.

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:08.390
Oryzae

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:03.960
So, whether it's Tadayasu,

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.540
Oikawa, Hasegawa-san, or Mutou-san,

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.390
let us brew in battle!

00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:10.000
Forward, men!

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:13.730
Shut up.

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:15.330
Who were you telling to shut up?

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:16.610
Oh... Uh...

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:22.790
By the way, Oikawa still doesn't

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:26.780
know that Tadayasu can see microbes.

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:29.290
All the other members know, though.

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:30.460
Did you find it?

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:33.270
The yeast for soy sauce, miso, and sake.

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:35.710
Yes. They were labeled.

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:37.960
Miso

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:37.960
Soy Sauce

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:37.960
Sake

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:42.720
Are you okay with keeping 
secrets from Oikawa, Tadayasu?

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:50.030
We had something for crushing beans?

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:53.120
The one in the farm resource 
center worked, so I brought it.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:56.180
Mutou-san, should I bring 
in the roasted wheat, too?

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:59.000
No, you can take it straight 
to the malt cellar.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.080
The soy sauce is in malt cellar 1.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:03.710
This is great. You're all hard workers.

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:05.160
Well done, well done.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:09.250
But if you work this hard from 
the start, you'll never last.

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:11.380
Preparing soy sauce and miso

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:14.170
takes four days and four 
nights of endless work.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:17.270
Four days and four nights?

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:18.410
In these clothes?

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:19.670
But I have classes!

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:22.260
Borrow clothes from Haruka-san.

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:23.710
The sexiest ones you can get.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:26.070
I'll endure it in my 
dirty clothes, thanks.

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:28.430
Oikawa is a microbe killer, after all.

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:29.600
I know!

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:32.430
Let us use the baths in 
your student-run dorm!

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:34.620
Oh, that might be a good idea.

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.050
There are showers here, too,

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:37.990
but since you're staying over anyway,

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:40.930
it'd be nice to have a long 
soak with all the girls.

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:47.240
Baths

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:46.780
Wow, Hazuki-chan, your skin is gorgeous!

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:49.200
Occupied

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:49.580
Oh, come on, yours is 
much smoother, Mutou-san!

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:51.890
But the real surprise is Yuuki-kun.

00:12:51.890 --> 00:12:53.080
He really looks like a girl.

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.830
C. trichoides

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.830
What are you doing? Please don't!

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:57.240
Oh, why not?

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:58.740
You've come this far, so you 
should be prepared to show us!

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:00.410
Just do it.

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:02.970
They're just going to do it anyway. 
It's what women do.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.300
It's impure.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:06.090
That's why I won't lend my bath to them.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:08.730
Why is Kei with them?

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:10.170
Is that even right?

00:13:10.170 --> 00:13:11.690
Stupid virgins.

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:13.600
They really are idiots.

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:15.390
Sawaki, you're going back to

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:17.060
the cellar instead of 
the apartment, right?

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:19.610
What, you guys aren't coming back?

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:22.360
Hasegawa-san said she had 
something to do and went home.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:25.350
I'm on duty starting 
at six. I gotta sleep.

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:26.810
I've had all I can take, too.

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:28.760
The rest of today is all yours, Sawaki.

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:30.870
Enjoy being surrounded by girls.

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:32.490
Just be careful.

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:34.600
I heard the dirty jokes are <i>really</i>

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:36.970
dirty when it's only women around.

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:39.090
You're better off playing innocent

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:40.840
than pretending you get it 
and humiliating yourself.

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:44.120
Did you learn that from 
some late-night anime,

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:45.510
or your own fantasies?

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:47.010
Malt Cellar 1

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:49.080
It's gonna burst!

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:50.930
Occupied

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:54.890
"Bursting" occurs when microbes diffuse throughout rice grains, turning it into malt rice.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:51.430
Yeah! It's gonna burst!

00:13:50.930 --> 00:14:00.930
Burst

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:53.170
Tadayasu, Oikawa and the others

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:56.160
come to stir up the rice every few hours,

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:00.680
so now we spend four days 
developing malt in all this rice!

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.030
After that, salt water will 
be poured into the barrel,

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:07.950
and the yeast fermentation becomes 
long-term secondary fermentation.

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:14.950
Soybeans (Protein)

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:14.950
Wheat (Starch)

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:14.950
Yeast

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:14.950
Amino Acids

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:10.080
The enzymes produced by the yeast

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:13.060
spend almost a year 
producing unrefined mash,

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:14.950
which becomes soy sauce.

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:18.210
Pure Brewed 
Dark 
Soy Sauce

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:19.600
The completed mash is pressed, 
pasteurized, and allowed to settle,

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.370
and then it becomes the standard 
soy sauce, "dark soy sauce."

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:24.920
Definitely a must-have 
in any Japanese kitchen.

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:27.470
You can't even begin to make 
Japanese food without this!

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:31.190
And the mash that isn't pasteurized 
becomes "pure soy sauce."

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:34.970
Pure Soy Sauce

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:35.320
This dark variety best preserves 
the original soy sauce flavor.

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:34.970
Best Preserved Soy Sauce Flavor

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:44.460
Light Soy Sauce

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:37.940
By the way, "light soy sauce" has a

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:40.200
paler color than standard soy sauce,

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:41.790
but the taste is not weaker.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:44.480
In fact, it has a 2% higher salt content.

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:46.490
So it's only the color that's lighter?

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:47.990
You guys spare no details...

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:53.680
Oh, Sawaki, are you off duty now?

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:54.370
Yeah.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:56.870
Then, let's go to the break room.

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:58.780
Like Professor Itsuki said,

00:14:58.780 --> 00:15:00.850
if you work too hard 
now, you'll never last.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:02.770
<i>Just be careful.</i>

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:04.950
<i>I heard the dirty jokes are </i>really<i> dirty</i>

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:06.760
<i>when it's only women around.</i>

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:10.580
Well, it should be okay if Kei's there.

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:11.480
What will?

00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:14.930
S. cerevisiae

00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:16.620
Oh...

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:18.940
Mutou-san fell asleep?

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:20.850
Yeah, I think she had 
too much to drink again.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:25.460
The Itsuki seminar really is fun.

00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:28.470
It has nothing to do with 
our classes, though.

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:30.360
Really?

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:33.220
Yeah, but the professor's so overzealous.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:34.780
He's always exaggerating, like...

00:15:35.180 --> 00:15:39.990
"Miso is the world's greatest 
masterpiece of solid fermentation!"

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:41.120
...and stuff like that.

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:43.960
It is true that a lot of the fermentation

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:46.080
done in Asia is solid fermentation.

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:47.680
Even with sake.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:51.090
Western liquors are primarily made by 
fermenting fruit juices and other liquids,

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:54.140
but many Asian liquors originate 
from non-liquid products.

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:57.690
You mean sake wasn't originally a drink?

00:15:57.690 --> 00:15:59.390
There were some types that weren't.

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:00.890
Grain

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:02.780
People used to put grain 
and yeast in a pot,

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:04.020
Fermentation

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:05.900
ferment the mixture without 
adding water, and eat it.

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:05.900
Fermentation

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:08.250
You sure know a lot, Yuuki-kun.

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:10.440
My family does own a brewery, after all.

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:12.910
They're having a pretty 
serious conversation...

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:14.940
Oh, yeah! One time...

00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:17.240
Awamori

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:17.240
I went to visit an awamori distillery.

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:17.240
Awamori is a strong liquor, unique to Okinawa, made from rice by distilling, not brewing.

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:22.210
Their awamori was classified for 
sale based on alcohol content.

00:16:22.710 --> 00:16:25.640
The window to the interior 
of the tank was marked

00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:27.780
indicating what brand it would become

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:30.410
if water was added to that point.

00:16:30.410 --> 00:16:33.030
I thought it was sort of funny 
how simple the technique was.

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:34.260
Wow!

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:37.480
But when I thought about it, 
it made sense.

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:41.270
Once it's distilled, awamori has 
a really high alcohol content.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:45.850
Wow, I didn't know they add water 
after it's already finished.

00:16:45.850 --> 00:16:47.640
I'm pretty sure you're not alone.

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:49.190
Is it the same with sake?

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:49.780
Yeah.

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:52.780
So the sake sold in stores 
is diluted with water?

00:16:53.060 --> 00:16:55.890
Well, after it's brewed, sake has an

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:58.030
alcohol content of around 20%.

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:00.690
Other than undiluted sake,

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:02.410
the types sold in stores are around 10%.

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:04.300
Y-Yeah, but...

00:17:04.300 --> 00:17:05.670
Wow, I didn't know that.

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:12.460
Through H.18

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:12.460
Sake with alcohol 
content between 
15% and 16% is 
not taxed.

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:13.300
Refined Sake

00:17:06.070 --> 00:17:07.280
There used to be a law that

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:09.810
sake with alcohol content higher than 15%

00:17:09.810 --> 00:17:10.970
was taxed increasingly higher

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:12.740
in accordance with increasing percentage.

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:15.240
But now, tax laws have changed,

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:17.710
so as long as the alcohol 
content is less than 22%,

00:17:17.710 --> 00:17:19.930
the tax applied is always the same.

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:21.700
Wow...

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:22.430
Wait.

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:26.180
I just thought of something really mean.

00:17:27.370 --> 00:17:30.980
Undiluted sake with 20% alcohol content

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:32.960
and sake that's diluted to 15%

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:34.610
have the same tax rate now, right?

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:37.930
So what's the point of diluting it?

00:17:37.930 --> 00:17:39.700
Wouldn't adding water inflate the cost?

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:43.820
A lot of people who love 
sake interpret it that way,

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:47.710
but undiluted sake with 20% 
alcohol content is strong stuff.

00:17:47.710 --> 00:17:48.970
Really?

00:17:48.970 --> 00:17:49.810
Yeah.

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:52.150
So, even though the 15%

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:54.760
alcohol content was set by the tax laws,

00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:55.890
if you look at it the other way,

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:58.510
it still has that familiar, 
strong sake flavor.

00:17:58.510 --> 00:17:59.630
Of course, now,

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:02.640
each brewery adjusts the dilution 
based on their own tastes.

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:01.930
I can't even keep up.

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:05.880
This goes beyond serious 
into just plain difficult.

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:05.160
If everyone sold only unprocessed sake,

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:09.980
it would raise the bar for 
all sake made in Japan.

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:09.980
What were Misato-san and 
Kawahama-san talking about?

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:11.150
I get it...

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:13.120
At this rate, there's hardly 
a place for you here.

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:15.190
That's not the problem.

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:16.490
Are you talking to yourself again?

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:18.330
Uh... No, I...

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:21.950
Oh, Sawaki!

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:23.950
Drink! Drink!

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:26.460
No, I'm underage, so...

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:31.210
You're no fun!

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:36.510
Oh, man, now you're soaked!

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:39.310
Want to borrow clothes from Hasegawa-san?

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:41.510
Uh, I'll pass...

00:18:41.510 --> 00:18:43.300
Actually, it's late anyway.

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:44.820
I think I'll head back to the store.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:46.320
Will you be okay like that?

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:48.330
Sure. See you tomorrow.

00:18:50.030 --> 00:18:52.050
Sawaki, I'll be fine from here.

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:52.920
See you.

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:54.780
Okay. Take care.

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:07.440
Oh!

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:09.290
Yuuki-kun left already?

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:10.490
Yeah.

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:13.360
But he couldn't have gone too far yet.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:15.820
He forgot his hairpiece.

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:17.300
Mutou-san was wearing it.

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:20.960
Yuuki-kun!

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:24.230
Yuuki-kun!

00:19:25.150 --> 00:19:26.060
Huh?

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:27.350
He's not there?

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:29.060
No. I wonder why...

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:38.200
It's the yeast from the sake 
that Mutou-san spilled.

00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:40.280
Oh well, too late now.

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:41.500
Tomorrow I'll—

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:48.200
Sawaki-kun, you knew about it?

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:49.690
About what?

00:19:49.690 --> 00:19:50.850
The opening!

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:52.770
Did Yuuki-kun leave through there?

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:53.460
Huh?

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:56.960
Hey, how do you open this wall?

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:02.900
I was just thinking the boxes 
aren't where they were before.

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:04.970
You know...

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:09.490
Sometimes I feel like I'm being 
kept in the dark about something.

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:14.580
That kind of thing really makes me mad.

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.950
Moyashimon

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.950
Returns

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.950
Episode 1

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:52.950
Microbe 
Theater

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:52.950
Microbe Theater Returns!

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:52.950
Returns

00:21:53.450 --> 00:21:56.870
A. oryzae

00:21:53.650 --> 00:21:55.500
Hi! A. oryzae here!

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:57.470
As the new series begins...

00:21:57.470 --> 00:22:00.580
Our Microbe Theater is back, too!

00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:03.620
In episode 1, we'll explain 
the term "Moyashiya,"

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:06.010
Moyashiya

00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:06.010
the origin of the name "Moyashimon."

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:07.920
A "moyashiya" is a kind of factory

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:11.760
that's indispensable in 
making fermented products.

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:12.910
Wait a minute!

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:19.190
S. cerevisiae

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:14.660
Oh, S. cerevisiae.

00:22:14.660 --> 00:22:16.600
Even alcoholic drinks 
originate from fruits and

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:18.980
other products that 
we yeasts have brewed!

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:22.650
That's true, alcohol naturally 
results from your guys' efforts,

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:24.420
but then some people showed up

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:26.430
who made sake from rice that we brewed,

00:22:26.070 --> 00:22:28.570
Sake

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:28.570
and from then on, they began to use malt.

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:32.350
Then, after some trial and error,

00:22:32.350 --> 00:22:34.320
the humans discovered "mold-starters."

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:35.040
Mold-starter factory

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:36.660
Factories that produced 
these mold-starters for sale

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:38.620
Moyashiya

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:38.620
came to be called "moyashiya."

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:40.810
By the way, the "moyashi" part

00:22:40.810 --> 00:22:43.210
comes from "moeru," meaning "to sprout,"

00:22:43.210 --> 00:22:44.800
from the way the malt microbes' spores

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:47.830
stretch out like flowers sprouting.

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:47.380
Moeru = Sprout

00:22:47.830 --> 00:22:49.700
Moe!
